# `core::primitives::valueless` -- `void` and `never`, the two built-ins that
# have no values at all.
#
# Grouped because that is the property they share and the reason both behave
# unlike every other primitive: there is nothing to construct, store, compare
# or pass. It is also why neither is *conformable* -- a spec method needs a
# `self` to receive, and neither type can supply one. They are declarable
# without being conformable, which is exactly the distinction between a
# primitive target and a conform target.

# The absence of a value, not a value that happens to be empty. A function's
# "returns nothing"; occupies no storage.
primitive void { }

# The bottom type: what an expression has when control never reaches past it.
# Legal only as a function, method, extern or gap's own declared return type
# (`extern exit : (code: i32) => never`), so it can never be the type of a
# binding, field or parameter. This declaration site is the single exception
# to that restriction.
primitive never { }
